Who answers what
The first answer (1A, institution category) decides how much of the form a firm sees. The XSD allows eight categories:
• Authorised payment institution (API)
• Small payment institution that has opted into safeguarding (SPI opt-in)
• Electronic money institution (EMI)
• EMI that also provides unrelated payment services (EMI + UPS)
• Small EMI (SEMI)
• SEMI opted into safeguarding for unrelated payment services (SEMI opt-in)
• Credit union that issues e-money
• Credit union opted into safeguarding for unrelated payment services (credit union opt-in)
Sections 1, 2 and 9 apply to everyone. Sections 3–8 open only when 5A ("required to safeguard relevant funds") is Yes. Sections 10 and 17 apply to EMI + UPS, SEMI opt-in and credit union opt-in only, and sections 11–16 open when 31A is Yes.
2. Platform landscape
REP027 is one handbook item inside DCP (the Data Collection Platform, also called REGDATA), which the delivery plan sizes at 72 microservices, 31 Lambdas, 10 shared libraries and 8 EMR Spark jobs. Only one of those services, dcp-sca-rep3-service, contains REP027 logic.
A save enters through Apigee and the Experience Online service, runs entirely inside dcp-sca-rep3-service, and writes to the S3 staging bucket and MySQL; DIMS, utility, CMS and submission services supply the return record, PDF rendering and submission.
Service names follow DCP's layers: Experience services are the UI-facing APIs, SCA services hold per-return application logic (REP027 sits in the REP3 service with REP026a/b/c), SCS services are shared core services (data items, utility, submission, schedule, CMS), and INT services integrate with external systems. Apigee authenticates the user and injects identity headers that every downstream service reads into a RequestContext.
