Database write patterns
FirmDataItemExtDaoImpl uses three patterns to survive re-enabled sections and concurrent saves:
• Update, then insert. batchUpsertSectionStatus and updateSectionStatuses run a batch UPDATE first, then INSERT only for sections where the update hit 0 rows. A 0-row update means DatabasePruningService.deleteSections removed the row when the section was hidden and the user has now re-enabled it.
• Insert-ignore shells. batchInsertIgnoreSectionsNotStarted uses INSERT IGNORE to create NOT STARTED rows, so two requests cannot create duplicates.
• Shell row for cross-section skips. updateCrossSectionSkippedQuesId inserts a NOT STARTED row carrying only SKIPPED_QUES_ID when the target section has no row yet.
Ordering and the Task JSON buffer
The rule is S3 first, database second, so a database row never points at answers that were not saved. After the 1 July snapshot, the team added TaskJsonBuffer (team notes, not in project snapshots): a per-request dirty write-back buffer that cuts S3 traffic from 4–6 round trips per save to one read and one write. It is a plain local object rather than a ThreadLocal, so data cannot leak between requests on pooled threads, and its flush runs in a finally block before the database sync.
