CSV upload pipeline
POST /v1/csv calls CsvParsingService.processCsvFile:
1.  validateCsvForQid and validateInputFile check that the file belongs to this question and is a usable CSV.
2.  CsvDefinitionFactory picks the Spring bean named after the file (lower-cased, without .csv). An unknown name fails with "Upload a file that matches the template for this section".
3.  parseCsv checks headers against getExpectedHeaders() (header text comes from csv.srf.* properties) and maps rows to DTOs with a Jackson MappingIterator. An empty file fails with "The file does not contain any data".
4.  pojoToXml2 wraps the rows with the matching XmlWrapper (SegregatedRelevantFundsXmlWrapper, AssetsXmlWrapper, CustodiansXmlWrapper, InsurerXmlWrapper, GuarantorsXmlWrapper) and builds an XML fragment.
5.  applicationService.schemaValidationAsync validates it with Saxon against schema/REP027/v1/REP027-Schema-csv.xsd, a cut-down schema that contains only Sections 4 and 12.
6.  On success, the file and a status record are saved to S3 and UploadStatusTracker is set to SUCCESS; on failure, markFailed stores the errors and the tracker says "Failed Validation".
The UI polls GET /v1/csv-status, can download the stored file with GET /v1/csv and remove it with DELETE /v1/csv. When a table question is skipped, DatabasePruningService.deleteRemovedCsvFiles deletes its CSV.
Delete row
Deleting a row in Section 4 (11–15) or Section 12 (37–41) touches four layers (team notes): the array in the answer JSON, the section-specific JSON, the ValidationTableError entries (which must be re-indexed so error row numbers still match), and the task JSON and FIRM_DATA_ITEM_EXT status. The uploaded CSV in S3 is left untouched by delete row.
