14. Engineering principles, pitfalls and open questions
Principles the team works by
• Drools owns behaviour. Branching, skip, visibility and business validation live in agenda groups; Java applies results and never re-implements a rule.
• Surgical fixes. Change the smallest thing that fixes the bug, test it, then move on; do not restructure working code while fixing.
• S3 before database. Flush the task JSON and answer JSON before syncing FIRM_DATA_ITEM_EXT, so the three stores never disagree in the database's favour.
• No ThreadLocal for request state. Request-scoped buffers are plain local objects so pooled threads cannot leak data between firms.
• Containers are not questions. Filter 2-argument QuestionsMap entries out before writing IDs to the database.
• Keep stale errors on purpose. Drools fires only the current page, so errors from other sections are carried forward by accumulateValidationErrors and cleared only when their own page is fixed.
• Idempotent inserts. Use INSERT IGNORE (or update-then-insert) for rows other requests may create at the same time.
